[{"id":"31321","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>When carbon dioxide is passed through lime water, which colour precipitate is obtained?</span></p>","option_1":"Red","option_2":"Green","option_3":"Blue","option_4":"White","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - White.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>When CO2 is passed through limewater (calcium hydroxide solution), it forms calcium carbonate, which is an insoluble white precipitate. This reaction (CO2 + Ca(OH)2 → CaCO3 + H2O) is the standard test for the presence of carbon dioxide gas.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31322","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What was 'Brahmadeya' mentioned in Chola inscriptions?</span></p>","option_1":"A type of land gifted to Brahmanas","option_2":"A type of tax","option_3":"A trading association","option_4":"A military assembly","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - A type of land gifted to Brahmanas.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Brahmadeya refers to land grants made tax-free to Brahmanas by Chola rulers and other patrons, recorded extensively in Chola inscriptions. These grants were meant to support Brahmanical learning and religious institutions and often formed the nucleus of new agrarian settlements.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31323","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In India, the coking coal mines were nationalised in the year ______.</span></p>","option_1":"1970","option_2":"1972","option_3":"1974","option_4":"1976","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 1972.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Coking coal mines in India were nationalised in 1972 under the Coking Coal Mines (Emergency Provisions) Act, 1971, followed by the Coking Coal Mines (Nationalisation) Act, 1972. Non-coking coal mines were nationalised later, in 1973.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31324","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In which region did the Langahs and Arghuns dominate extensively before they were subdued by the Mughals?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. Multan</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. Sindh</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Langah dynasty ruled over Multan and the Arghun dynasty controlled Sindh before both regions were brought under Mughal control (Multan was annexed under Akbar's predecessors, and Sindh was fully subdued by Akbar). Hence both statements I and II are correct.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31325","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Mudrarakshas', a play that gives insight into the Mauryan period, was written by ______.</span></p>","option_1":"Bhasa","option_2":"Chanakya","option_3":"Banabhatta","option_4":"Visakhadatta","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Visakhadatta.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Mudrarakshasa is a Sanskrit political drama written by Vishakhadatta. It narrates the political maneuvers of Chanakya to help Chandragupta Maurya secure the throne of Magadha by winning over the minister Rakshasa, offering valuable insight into the early Mauryan period.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31326","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The folk dance 'Chheih Lam' is traditionally performed in which of the following Indian states?</span></p>","option_1":"Goa","option_2":"Mizoram","option_3":"Telangana","option_4":"Karnataka","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Mizoram.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Chheih Lam is a traditional folk dance of Mizoram, usually performed during festive occasions where dancers move in a line or circle to the rhythm of songs, often accompanying festivals like Chapchar Kut.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31327","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The 'Lai-Haraoba' festival is celebrated in which Indian state?</span></p>","option_1":"Manipur","option_2":"Tamil Nadu","option_3":"Gujarat","option_4":"Himachal Pradesh","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Manipur.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Lai Haraoba is a pre-Hindu Meitei festival of Manipur, meaning 'the merrymaking of the gods'. It involves ritual dances and songs performed to please local deities (Umang Lai) and is considered a precursor to classical Manipuri dance.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31328","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In which year Rana Pratap ascended the throne of Mewar?</span></p>","option_1":"1556","option_2":"1561","option_3":"1572","option_4":"1581","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 1572.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Maharana Pratap ascended the throne of Mewar in 1572 after the death of his father, Maharana Udai Singh II. He is best remembered for the Battle of Haldighati (1576) fought against the Mughal forces under Akbar.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31329","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Gurpurab' festival of Punjab is celebrated in honour of the ______.</span></p>","option_1":"Hindu Gods","option_2":"Sikh Gurus","option_3":"Sufi Saints","option_4":"Buddhist Monks","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Sikh Gurus.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Gurpurab refers to celebrations associated with the lives of the Sikh Gurus, most notably the birth anniversaries of Guru Nanak Dev and Guru Gobind Singh. These occasions are marked by processions, kirtan, and langar.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31330","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Vikram lander and Pragyan rover is associated with ______.</span></p>","option_1":"Chandrayaan-1","option_2":"Chandrayaan-3","option_3":"Mangalyaan","option_4":"Aditya L-1","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Chandrayaan-3.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Vikram lander and Pragyan rover were part of India's Chandrayaan-3 mission, launched by ISRO in July 2023. Vikram successfully soft-landed near the Moon's south pole in August 2023, making India the first country to achieve this feat.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31331","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>As per census 2011, almost half of India's population lives in just ______ states.</span></p>","option_1":"3","option_2":"5","option_3":"11","option_4":"9","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 5.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>According to Census 2011 data, nearly half of India's population is concentrated in just five states — Uttar Pradesh, Maharashtra, Bihar, West Bengal, and Madhya Pradesh — highlighting the uneven distribution of population across the country.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31332","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What is the change of state directly from solid to gas without changing into liquid state known as?</span></p>","option_1":"Solidification","option_2":"Sublimation","option_3":"Freezing","option_4":"Evaporation","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Sublimation.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The direct transition of a substance from the solid to the gaseous state without passing through the liquid phase is called sublimation. Common examples include camphor, naphthalene balls, and solid carbon dioxide (dry ice).</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31333","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>How many consecutive flight-tests of the 'Pralay' missile did DRDO conduct successfully in July 2025?</span></p>","option_1":"Two","option_2":"Three","option_3":"Four","option_4":"Five","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Two.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>DRDO successfully conducted two consecutive flight-tests of the short-range, surface-to-surface Pralay missile in July 2025, validating its accuracy, range, and reliability as part of ongoing efforts to induct the system into the armed forces.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31334","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which submarine-launched ballistic missile was highlighted as a game-changing launch by DRDO in November 2024?</span></p>","option_1":"Rudra","option_2":"K-4","option_3":"Dhruv","option_4":"Arjun","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - K-4.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In November 2024, DRDO carried out a significant test of the K-4 submarine-launched ballistic missile (SLBM), which has a strike range of around 3,500 km and strengthens India's nuclear triad by enhancing sea-based second-strike capability.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31335","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Among the following major port in India, which is located southernmost?</span></p>","option_1":"Mormugao Port","option_2":"Jawaharlal Nehru Port","option_3":"New Mangalore Port","option_4":"Deendayal Port","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - New Mangalore Port.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Among the given options, New Mangalore Port, located in Karnataka on the west coast, lies furthest south. Mormugao (Goa), Jawaharlal Nehru Port (Maharashtra), and Deendayal Port (Gujarat) are all located progressively further north along the west coast.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31336","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>As of October 2024, India's foreign exchange (forex) reserves have swelled to $______ billion.</span></p>","option_1":"701","option_2":"8100","option_3":"71","option_4":"7100","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 701.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>India's foreign exchange reserves touched an all-time high of around $701 billion in late September/October 2024, driven by strong capital inflows and the RBI's active reserve accumulation, before easing slightly in subsequent months.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31337","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following is correct regarding Granite?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. Granite is used in monuments.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. Granite is a major mineral as defined under Section 3(e) of MMDR Act, 1957, and as per Section 15 of MMDR Act, 1957.</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Only I.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Granite is indeed widely used for monuments, flooring, and construction due to its durability. However, under the Mines and Minerals (Development and Regulation) Act, 1957, granite used as building/dimension stone is classified as a minor mineral (under Section 3(e) read with state rules), not a major mineral, so statement II is incorrect. Hence only statement I is correct.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31338","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In 2019, a new Rs. 20 coin was announced in the budget. This coin is made up of ______.</span></p>","option_1":"copper, zinc and nickel","option_2":"copper, zinc and aluminium","option_3":"Stainless steel and copper","option_4":"Copper, bronze and silver","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - copper, zinc and nickel.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The new Rs. 20 coin introduced in 2019 is a twelve-sided coin made of an alloy of copper, zinc, and nickel, designed to be easily distinguishable by touch for the visually impaired.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31339","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Kho-Kho game was first introduced in the National Games of India in ______.</span></p>","option_1":"1935","option_2":"1965","option_3":"1985","option_4":"1975","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 1985.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Kho-Kho, though it originated much earlier in Maharashtra, was formally introduced as a competitive event at the National Games of India in 1985, after which it gained wider organised recognition across the country.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31340","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In which year Beijing was host city of Summer Olympic Games?</span></p>","option_1":"2000","option_2":"2004","option_3":"2008","option_4":"2012","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 2008.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Beijing hosted the Summer Olympic Games in 2008, becoming the first Chinese city to do so. Beijing later also hosted the Winter Olympics in 2022, making it the first city to host both Summer and Winter Games.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31341","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following books has been written by Devdutt Pattanaik?</span></p>","option_1":"Jonaki","option_2":"My Gita","option_3":"Tomb of Sand","option_4":"The Patient Assassin","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - My Gita.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'My Gita' is a well-known book authored by Devdutt Pattanaik, a mythologist and author known for his works simplifying Indian mythology. 'Jonaki' is by Rabindranath Tagore-inspired works/other authors, 'Tomb of Sand' is by Geetanjali Shree, and 'The Patient Assassin' is by Anita Anand.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31342","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The term \"Circle Centre\" and \"three-point line\" are related to which sports?</span></p>","option_1":"Badminton","option_2":"Basketball","option_3":"Football","option_4":"Kho-kho","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Basketball.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Circle Centre' and the 'three-point line' are terms specific to Basketball. The three-point line marks the distance beyond which a successful field goal is worth three points instead of two, and the centre circle is used for the opening jump ball.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31343","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Flagposts, Nets and Stick are the few terms related to which of the following sports?</span></p>","option_1":"Cricket","option_2":"Badminton","option_3":"Hockey","option_4":"Football","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Hockey.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Flagposts, nets (goal nets), and sticks are all standard equipment terms associated with the game of Hockey, which uses a stick to hit the ball and flagposts to mark field boundaries.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31344","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following is correct regarding the Buddhist Stupas?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. They were built of huge mounds of mud.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. They were enclosed in carefully burnt small standard bricks.</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Early Buddhist stupas were typically built as huge earthen/mud mounds, which were then encased and reinforced with carefully burnt, standard-sized bricks or stone to give them a finished, durable structure. Hence both statements I and II are correct.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"31345","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"842","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which pair of elements are isobars?</span></p>","option_1":"Oxygen and Carbon","option_2":"Argon and Calcium","option_3":"Sodium and Magnesium","option_4":"Carbon and Nitrogen","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Argon and Calcium.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Isobars are atoms of different elements having the same mass number but different atomic numbers. Argon (mass number 40, atomic number 18) and Calcium (mass number 40, atomic number 20) both have a mass number of 40, making them isobars.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""}]